  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Find $PQ,$ if\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $PQ=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |",
  "worked_solution": "Here, $P$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix and $Q$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix.  \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $P$ and $1$ row in matrix $Q$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{P}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{\u2713}{Q}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={PQ}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot (-4) & 0\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-4) & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-4) & (-1)\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
